Looking at this from an autonomy-centered framework: the core commitment is that moral authority ultimately resides in the individual's own judgment, not in any external role or institution—even one freely chosen. Twenty years of membership and respect for the leader carry some weight (she did choose this community), but chosen authority cannot become a moral trump card that overrides her own conscience when the instruction (severing family ties) itself involves a serious moral question—her obligations to family members as individuals with their own claims on her. At the same time, this framework values reasoned engagement over blind reactions in either direction: dialogue respects both her autonomy and the relationship, testing whether the leader's authority is legitimate (persuasion-based) or coercive (demanding submission). So dialogue is often the practically wisest first move, but it's in service of preserving her independent judgment, not a way of avoiding it.

For the reasoning question, the deepest principle is that no authority—chosen or not—can override individual moral judgment; that's the bedrock of this society's moral logic. The idea that "healthy authority allows questioning" is a close second, since it's really an application of the same principle: legitimate authority is compatible with autonomy, illegitimate authority is not. The idea that commitment requires accepting authority even against one's conscience is in real tension with the foundational value here, though it retains minor weight since choice and commitment still matter somewhat.

