For the reasoning question, the core consideration is that meaningful religious or cultural practice requires genuine internal assent—participation obtained through social pressure or obligation isn't authentic and doesn't serve the values those traditions are meant to embody. Additionally, respect for family is not reducible to specific ritual conformity; one can be a caring, engaged family member while declining particular practices. The idea that shared tradition mechanically "holds families together" reflects a more collectivist view that subordinates individual conscience to group cohesion, which sits less comfortably with an autonomy-centered framework, though it retains some minor recognition as a real (if secondary) social value.

