For the second question, the autonomy view resists the idea that "family members have a right to count on each other" in a way that binds someone's major life decisions—this frames obligation too collectively for a framework centered on individual rights. The idea that education "ultimately serves the family too" is a possible instrumental justification, but it's somewhat beside the point since his right to choose doesn't depend on the choice benefiting others. The core consideration is that he alone has the standing to decide his path, given that no one else can rightfully claim to determine it for him.

