For the second question, similarly, the deepest explanatory principle is that identity is self-constructed—people are authors of their own lives. The fact that one can belong to a community without being defined by it is a true and important corollary, but it is downstream of the more basic idea that self-construction is what identity fundamentally is. The claim that we are essentially shaped by our origins runs counter to the autonomy framework's central commitment, though it retains minor descriptive validity (people do emerge from particular contexts) even if it shouldn't be given normative authority.

